#!/usr/bin/env bash
# hmd-exec — single dispatcher for "run a headless coding task."
#
# THE GAP IT CLOSES. Every autonomous hmd path (decompose, session-fork, the maintainer
# loop, the fallback-routed fix child, ...) independently shells out to `claude -p`. That
# means "run a headless coding task" has N slightly-different call shapes instead of one,
# and swapping the harness under any of them means editing N places. This introduces the
# ONE seam: a caller asks hmd-exec to run a task; hmd-exec picks a backend and runs it.
#
# WAVE 1 (this file, today): ONE backend — `claude-code` — and it is BYTE-IDENTICAL to the
# direct `hmd_claude_retry`/`claude -p` spawn it replaces. Nothing about behaviour changes;
# only the seam exists now. See docs/analysis/2026-08-25-harness-independence-design.md
# (Stage 0/1) for the staged path this sets up.
#
# WAVE 2 (bin/lib/hmd_api_backend.py): a `--backend api` runner that talks to an
# Anthropic-shaped /v1/messages endpoint directly, with no Claude Code dependency. It is a
# COMPLETION-ONLY backend, by deliberate, documented choice: no tool-use, no file edits, no
# bash, no multi-step loop. It refuses loudly (exit 3) whenever a caller's --allowedTools
# says the task needs tool-use, and refuses loudly (exit 4) whenever bin/heimdall-fallback's
# gate does not return a ROUTE verdict for the calling repo — no default route, no bypass.
# See "THE WAVE-2 SEAM" below for the contract, and bin/lib/hmd_api_backend.py's own header
# for the full, honest "what this cannot do" list.
#
# USAGE:
#   hmd-exec [--backend NAME] [--strict] run <claude-shaped argv...>
#   hmd-exec [--backend NAME] backends
#   hmd-exec -h | --help
#
#   Global flags come before the subcommand (matches bin/heimdall-pressure /
#   bin/heimdall-metric / bin/heimdall-fallback). Everything after the `run` token is
#   passed through to the backend byte-for-byte, UNEXAMINED by hmd-exec's own parser —
#   so a prompt that happens to contain text shaped like an hmd-exec flag (e.g. a prompt
#   discussing "--backend") is never misparsed: hmd-exec stops looking for its own flags
#   the instant it consumes the `run` token.
#
# BACKEND SELECTION — explicit and observable (repo convention: never guess silently):
#   * Explicit: `--backend NAME` / `--backend=NAME`, else $HMD_EXEC_BACKEND, else
#     "claude-code". Precedence is flag > env > default, same order as MODEL/WORK_DIR
#     resolution in bin/session-fork.
#   * Observable: every dispatch announces `hmd-exec: backend=<name>` on STDERR (never
#     stdout — callers do `output=$(hmd-exec ...)` and stdout must stay exactly the
#     wrapped task's real output, nothing else). `hmd-exec backends` lists the full
#     roster + implementation status + which one is currently selected, for humans/CI.
#   * An UNKNOWN --backend value is ALWAYS a hard, loud failure (exit 2), unconditionally
#     — this is a deliberate divergence from heimdall-pressure/metric/fallback's "argument
#     errors are a soft 0 unless --strict" convention. Those three tools are side-channel
#     telemetry/gates: their stdout is never the actual task's payload, so a silent no-op
#     costs a missed log line at worst. hmd-exec's stdout ON SUCCESS *is* the task's real
#     output — silently substituting a default backend (or worse, a silent no-op with
#     exit 0 and empty stdout) is exactly the swallowed-failure class
#     bin/lib/hmd-claude-retry.sh's own header exists to prevent. Correctness here always
#     outranks convention parity.
#
# --strict: accepted for CLI-shape parity with the three tools above (global flag, opt-in,
#   tests-only). Its ONE real effect: `run` with zero passthrough args (a caller bug — no
#   task was actually given) is rejected under --strict, and tolerated (passed through
#   anyway) by default, matching those tools' "never fail a caller over a usage edge case"
#   default. It does NOT gate backend-name validation — see above for why that stays
#   unconditional.
#
# THE WAVE-2 SEAM — the interface bin/lib/hmd_api_backend.py implements. To be a drop-in
# replacement for `claude-code` for the tasks it CAN do, a backend receiving the argv after
# `run` (shaped like
# `-p <prompt> [--model <id>] [--output-format <fmt>] [--permission-mode <mode>]
#  [--allowedTools <csv>]`) must:
#   1. Treat `-p <prompt>` as the task; flags it has no equivalent for may be ignored,
#      but `-p`'s argument must never be misinterpreted as anything but the task prompt.
#   2. On success, write ONLY the task's final answer to stdout — no banners, no logs
#      merged in (callers capture stdout verbatim as `output=$(hmd-exec ...)`).
#   3. Exit 0 on success. On a transient/overload-shaped failure after exhausting its own
#      retry budget, exit with $HMD_OVERLOAD_EXIT (default 75) so existing callers that
#      branch on that sentinel keep working unmodified. Any OTHER non-zero exit is a real,
#      fail-fast error — never silently retried, never masked.
#   4. Retry/backoff strategy is the backend's own concern; hmd-exec imposes no HOW, only
#      the exit-code/stdout shape above.
# bin/lib/hmd_api_backend.py satisfies all four AND adds its own, honestly-documented
# refusal paths (no tool-use capability, no route without the fallback gate's ROUTE
# verdict) — see that file's header for the full contract, including exit codes 2-4 which
# are additive detail on top of the "any other nonzero = real error" rule above, never a
# replacement for it.
set -eo pipefail

SELF="$0"
if command -v readlink &>/dev/null; then
  SELF="$(readlink -f "$0" 2>/dev/null || readlink "$0" 2>/dev/null || echo "$0")"
fi
PLUGIN_DIR="$(cd "$(dirname "$SELF")/.." && pwd)"

_HMD_EXEC_BACKENDS="claude-code api"

usage() {
  cat <<'EOF'
hmd-exec — single dispatcher for a headless coding task (Wave 1: seam only; the
claude-code backend is byte-identical to today's direct `claude -p` spawns).

Usage:
  hmd-exec [--backend NAME] [--strict] run <claude-shaped argv...>
  hmd-exec [--backend NAME] backends
  hmd-exec -h | --help

Global flags (before the subcommand):
  --backend NAME   which backend runs the task: claude-code (default) | api
                    (default also settable via $HMD_EXEC_BACKEND)
  --strict         tests only; see the header comment for exactly what this gates

Subcommands:
  run <argv...>    dispatch a headless coding task to the selected backend.
                    Everything after `run` is passed through byte-for-byte.
  backends         list available backends, status, and which one is selected.

Backends:
  claude-code   (default, implemented) — shells to `claude -p` via the existing
                529-discriminating retry wrapper (bin/lib/hmd-claude-retry.sh).
                Reused verbatim, never reimplemented.
  api           (implemented, completion-only) — a single Anthropic-shaped
                /v1/messages call via bin/lib/hmd_api_backend.py, routed only
                through bin/heimdall-fallback's gate (no gate ROUTE verdict = no
                call, no bypass). NO tool-use: refuses (exit 3) any task whose
                --allowedTools is non-empty. See that file's header for the full
                "what this cannot do" list.
EOF
}

BACKEND="${HMD_EXEC_BACKEND:-claude-code}"
STRICT=0
SAW_SUBCOMMAND=""

while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    --backend) BACKEND="${2:?--backend needs a NAME}"; shift 2 ;;
    --backend=*) BACKEND="${1#--backend=}"; shift ;;
    --strict) STRICT=1; shift ;;
    -h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;;
    run)
      SAW_SUBCOMMAND="run"
      shift
      break
      ;;
    backends)
      SAW_SUBCOMMAND="backends"
      shift
      break
      ;;
    *)
      echo "hmd-exec: unknown option or missing subcommand: $1" >&2
      usage >&2
      exit 2
      ;;
  esac
done

if [ -z "$SAW_SUBCOMMAND" ]; then
  echo "hmd-exec: missing subcommand (expected 'run' or 'backends')" >&2
  usage >&2
  exit 2
fi

if [ "$SAW_SUBCOMMAND" = "backends" ]; then
  for b in $_HMD_EXEC_BACKENDS; do
    case "$b" in
      claude-code) note="implemented, default" ;;
      api)         note="implemented, completion-only (no tool-use; see bin/lib/hmd_api_backend.py)" ;;
      *)           note="unknown backend (no status registered)" ;;
    esac
    [ "$b" = "$BACKEND" ] && note="$note [selected]"
    printf '%s — %s\n' "$b" "$note"
  done
  exit 0
fi

# SAW_SUBCOMMAND = run. Everything left in "$@" is the backend's argv, untouched.
if [ "$STRICT" -eq 1 ] && [ $# -eq 0 ]; then
  echo "hmd-exec: --strict run given zero task args — no task to run" >&2
  exit 2
fi

printf 'hmd-exec: backend=%s\n' "$BACKEND" >&2

case "$BACKEND" in
  claude-code)
    # shellcheck source=lib/hmd-claude-retry.sh
    . "$PLUGIN_DIR/bin/lib/hmd-claude-retry.sh"
    # `|| rc=$?` (never a bare call) is load-bearing, not style: hmd_claude_retry's own
    # retry loop does an UNPROTECTED `out="$(...)"; rc=$?` internally. Under this script's
    # `set -e`, calling the function bare lets the first overloaded attempt's non-zero
    # status trip errexit before the library's own retry logic ever runs — verified
    # empirically that bin/decompose has exactly this latent bug today (bare call under
    # its own `set -e`: the first 529 kills the script instead of retrying). Wrapping the
    # call so its status is "tested" suspends errexit for the whole call, matching the
    # protected style bin/session-fork already uses safely.
    rc=0
    hmd_claude_retry "$@" || rc=$?
    exit "$rc"
    ;;
  api)
    # A completion-only backend (bin/lib/hmd_api_backend.py's own header explains the
    # (a)-vs-(b) choice in full). `exec` hands off the process image directly, so its
    # exit code IS this script's exit code — no `|| rc=$?` dance needed here, unlike
    # the claude-code case above, because there is no bash retry loop's errexit
    # interaction to protect against: any retrying happens inside the python process.
    exec "$PLUGIN_DIR/bin/lib/hmd_api_backend.py" "$@"
    ;;
  *)
    echo "hmd-exec: unknown backend '$BACKEND' (available: $_HMD_EXEC_BACKENDS)" >&2
    exit 2
    ;;
esac
